{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-24:39\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The matching arrival-and-finding frame makes 24:39 a useful counter-scene: the apparent water vanishes, while the seeker finds God and his account; here Dhu al-Qarnayn finds a people by the spring. The verb وَجَدَ presents what is found from the traveler’s vantage, without making the scene a universal cosmographic claim.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ وَجَدَهَا تَغْرُبُ فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"24:39\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا جَآءَهُۥ لَمْ يَجِدْهُ شَيْـًۭٔا وَوَجَدَ ٱللَّهَ عِندَهُۥ فَوَفَّىٰهُ حِسَابَهُۥ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both scenes use حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا and وَجَدَ at an arrival, and both place the finding at عِندَهَا/عِندَهُ.\",\"limits\":\"The shared wording does not establish a direct allusion or prove that the spring is an illusion.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-24:55\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The divine promise to establish believers in the earth may illuminate the focus’s statement that God enabled Dhu al-Qarnayn there; both use the root م ك ن for earthly establishment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:84\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّا مَكَّنَّا لَهُۥ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ\"},{\"ref\":\"24:55\",\"ar\":\"وَعَدَ ٱللَّهُ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ مِنكُمْ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ لَيَسْتَخْلِفَنَّهُمْ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ كَمَا ٱسْتَخْلَفَ ٱلَّذِينَ مِن قَبْلِهِمْ وَلَيُمَكِّنَنَّ لَهُمْ دِينَهُمُ ٱلَّذِى ٱرْتَضَىٰ لَهُمْ\"}],\"missing\":\"The wording supports a shared idea of God-given establishment, but not that this communal promise specifically describes Dhu al-Qarnayn. A passage identifying his faith or linking his rule to this promise could establish that application.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-24:58\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-24:59\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-25:45\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:74\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:134\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The recurring phrase gardens and springs describes abundance in two different prophet stories, whereas the focus gives one muddy spring at a journey’s edge. This may contrast cultivated provision with a threshold scene.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:134\",\"ar\":\"وَجَنَّٰتٍۢ وَعُيُونٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:147\",\"ar\":\"فِى جَنَّٰتٍۢ وَعُيُونٍۢ\"}],\"missing\":\"The focus does not say whether its people draw provision from or cultivate around the spring. Further description of their relation to it, or Quranic occurrences contrasting عين حمئة with fertile springs, could establish a deliberate contrast.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:147\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"same-as:R-26:134\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:15\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"David and Solomon explicitly answer divinely given knowledge with praise. The focus likewise precedes Dhu al-Qarnayn’s journey with God’s enabling and provision, though it does not narrate his gratitude.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:84\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّا مَكَّنَّا لَهُۥ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَءَاتَيْنَٰهُ مِن كُلِّ شَىْءٍۢ سَبَبًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"27:15\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَدْ ءَاتَيْنَا دَاوُۥدَ وَسُلَيْمَٰنَ عِلْمًۭا ۖ وَقَالَا ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِى فَضَّلَنَا عَلَىٰ كَثِيرٍۢ مِّنْ عِبَادِهِ ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both narratives describe a ruler’s capacity as received from God.\",\"limits\":\"The knowledge and praise in 27:15 are not attributed to Dhu al-Qarnayn.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:16\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Solomon’s statement that he was given much parallels the focus’s report that Dhu al-Qarnayn was given means for his journey; each frames extraordinary capacity as a gift.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:84\",\"ar\":\"وَءَاتَيْنَٰهُ مِن كُلِّ شَىْءٍۢ سَبَبًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"27:16\",\"ar\":\"وَوَرِثَ سُلَيْمَٰنُ دَاوُۥدَ ۖ وَقَالَ يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلنَّاسُ عُلِّمْنَا مَنطِقَ ٱلطَّيْرِ وَأُوتِينَا مِن كُلِّ شَىْءٍ ۖ إِنَّ هَٰذَا لَهُوَ ٱلْفَضْلُ ٱلْمُبِينُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus introduces Dhu al-Qarnayn’s expeditions with God’s enabling and a means for each one.\",\"limits\":\"The shared gift language does not equate the two rulers or their particular gifts.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:24\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The two stories share a striking discovery pattern: وَجَدَ followed by a feminine referent and a people. In 27:24 the discovered woman and her people worship the sun; the focus reports a people by the setting sun but leaves their beliefs unstated. The parallel invites comparison while preserving that silence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"وَجَدَهَا تَغْرُبُ فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"27:24\",\"ar\":\"وَجَدتُّهَا وَقَوْمَهَا يَسْجُدُونَ لِلشَّمْسِ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages join a solar setting or worship scene with a ruler’s discovery of a people.\",\"limits\":\"The focus never says its people worship the sun; the parallel does not identify the communities or their conduct.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:4\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Pharaoh’s rule divides and violently oppresses a people, while Dhu al-Qarnayn’s response is framed conditionally: wrongdoing receives punishment, and faith with good action receives a favorable response. The contrast presents power over a people as ethically accountable.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"أَمَّا مَن ظَلَمَ فَسَوْفَ نُعَذِّبُهُۥ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:88\",\"ar\":\"وَأَمَّا مَنْ ءَامَنَ وَعَمِلَ صَٰلِحًۭا فَلَهُۥ جَزَآءً ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"28:4\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ فِرْعَوْنَ عَلَا فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَجَعَلَ أَهْلَهَا شِيَعًۭا يَسْتَضْعِفُ طَآئِفَةًۭ مِّنْهُمْ يُذَبِّحُ أَبْنَآءَهُمْ وَيَسْتَحْىِۦ نِسَآءَهُمْ ۚ إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ مِنَ ٱلْمُفْسِدِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s paired choices are immediately explained in terms of how people act; Pharaoh’s rule supplies a stark contrary model of power.\",\"limits\":\"The verses do not explicitly name Dhu al-Qarnayn as Pharaoh’s foil.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:9\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:14\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:23\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Moses arrives at water and finds people drawing from it, then notices two women held back from the crowd and provides help. This scene gives a concrete social counterpart to Dhu al-Qarnayn finding a people beside a spring and facing a choice about how to treat them.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"وَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"28:23\",\"ar\":\"وَلَمَّا وَرَدَ مَآءَ مَدْيَنَ وَجَدَ عَلَيْهِ أُمَّةًۭ مِّنَ ٱلنَّاسِ يَسْقُونَ وَوَجَدَ مِن دُونِهِمُ ٱمْرَأَتَيْنِ تَذُودَانِ\"},{\"ref\":\"28:24\",\"ar\":\"فَسَقَىٰ لَهُمَا ثُمَّ تَوَلَّىٰٓ إِلَى ٱلظِّلِّ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both travel scenes bring an arriving traveler to water and a human community; Moses’s response to vulnerable people makes the focus’s goodness option tangible.\",\"limits\":\"Moses is a traveler helping two women, not a ruler deciding the treatment of a whole people.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:27\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:14\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-34:10\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"David receives فضل from God, followed by commands to make protective armor and act righteously. This offers a thin parallel to the focus’s sequence of God-given capacity followed by a consequential use of authority.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:84\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّا مَكَّنَّا لَهُۥ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَءَاتَيْنَٰهُ مِن كُلِّ شَىْءٍۢ سَبَبًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"34:10\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَدْ ءَاتَيْنَا دَاوُۥدَ مِنَّا فَضْلًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"34:11\",\"ar\":\"أَنِ ٱعْمَلْ سَٰبِغَٰتٍۢ وَقَدِّرْ فِى ٱلسَّرْدِ ۖ وَٱعْمَلُوا۟ صَٰلِحًا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus likewise attributes Dhu al-Qarnayn’s means to God before recounting his decisions among people.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not explicitly call Dhu al-Qarnayn’s actions righteous or connect them with David’s craft.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-34:12\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The noun عَيْن also names Solomon’s source of molten copper. That usage keeps the source sense of عَيْن available in the focus, where حَمِئَة anchors it as a muddy spring.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"34:12\",\"ar\":\"وَأَسَلْنَا لَهُۥ عَيْنَ ٱلْقِطْرِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same noun occurs in another account of a ruler’s journey of power, but with copper rather than mud.\",\"limits\":\"The two sources differ in substance and narrative function; the shared noun does not make them the same kind of spring.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-37:5\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-37:48\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-37:72\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-37:97\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-37:102\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-38:20\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"David’s strengthened kingdom and granted wisdom provide a broad parallel for Dhu al-Qarnayn’s God-enabled authority, while adding no specific guidance about the choice in this ayah.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:84\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّا مَكَّنَّا لَهُۥ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ\"},{\"ref\":\"38:20\",\"ar\":\"وَشَدَدْنَا مُلْكَهُۥ وَءَاتَيْنَٰهُ ٱلْحِكْمَةَ وَفَصْلَ ٱلْخِطَابِ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both accounts explicitly attribute a ruler’s power or capacity to God.\",\"limits\":\"The focus names no wisdom or judgment quality corresponding to David’s فصل الخطاب.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-38:26\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The command to David to judge among people by truth gives a close norm for reading Dhu al-Qarnayn’s alternatives. His reply applies punishment to wrongdoing and favor to faith with good action, making the decision a matter of conduct rather than indiscriminate rule.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"أَمَّا مَن ظَلَمَ فَسَوْفَ نُعَذِّبُهُۥ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:88\",\"ar\":\"وَأَمَّا مَنْ ءَامَنَ وَعَمِلَ صَٰلِحًۭا فَلَهُۥ جَزَآءً ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"38:26\",\"ar\":\"يَٰدَاوُۥدُ إِنَّا جَعَلْنَٰكَ خَلِيفَةًۭ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ فَٱحْكُم بَيْنَ ٱلنَّاسِ بِٱلْحَقِّ وَلَا تَتَّبِعِ ٱلْهَوَىٰ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages address authority over people and constrain its exercise by a standard of right conduct.\",\"limits\":\"David receives an explicit command to judge by truth; no identical formula is stated to Dhu al-Qarnayn.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-38:38\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The title ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ and مُقَرَّنِينَ share the root ق ر ن; the latter clearly describes people joined together in fetters. This may be relevant when weighing interpretations of the title’s dual form.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ\"},{\"ref\":\"38:38\",\"ar\":\"وَءَاخَرِينَ مُقَرَّنِينَ فِى ٱلْأَصْفَادِ\"}],\"missing\":\"A lexical account of ق ر ن and other Quranic uses of the title is needed to establish whether the sense of joining informs its meaning; the shared root alone does not settle what the two qarns are.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-40:36\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus says Dhu al-Qarnayn was given a sabab and followed it until he reached the sun’s setting place. Pharaoh also boasts that he will reach the asbāb, including the routes of the heavens. The repeated sabab/balagh language contrasts God-enabled travel through the earth with a ruler’s boastful attempt to reach the heavens.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:84\",\"ar\":\"وَءَاتَيْنَٰهُ مِن كُلِّ شَىْءٍۢ سَبَبًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:85\",\"ar\":\"فَأَتْبَعَ سَبَبًا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ\"},{\"ref\":\"40:36\",\"ar\":\"لَعَلِّىٓ أَبْلُغُ ٱلْأَسْبَٰبَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages link reaching with asbāb; 40:37 specifies Pharaoh’s intended ascent toward the God of Moses.\",\"limits\":\"The verbal echo does not itself establish a deliberate allusion or make Dhu al-Qarnayn’s journey supernatural.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-42:15\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The command to the Messenger to be just among his hearers supplies a general ethical counterpart to Dhu al-Qarnayn’s decision over a people, but not a direct rule for his particular alternatives.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"42:15\",\"ar\":\"وَأُمِرْتُ لِأَعْدِلَ بَيْنَكُمُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus places a decision over people under divine speech; 42:15 makes justice an explicit command in another address.\",\"limits\":\"The speakers and situations differ, and the focus does not use the word عدل.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-42:40\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The paired rule of proportionate response and forgiveness that repairs offers a close ethical frame for Dhu al-Qarnayn’s punishment-or-goodness choice. It clarifies that the constructive branch matters, while not reducing حُسْنًا to pardon alone.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"42:40\",\"ar\":\"وَجَزَٰٓؤُا۟ سَيِّئَةٍۢ سَيِّئَةٌۭ مِّثْلُهَا ۖ فَمَنْ عَفَا وَأَصْلَحَ فَأَجْرُهُۥ عَلَى ٱللَّهِ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages set punitive response alongside a constructive alternative; the focus’s next verses sort treatment by wrongdoing or faith and good action.\",\"limits\":\"42:40 addresses response to harm and does not prescribe Dhu al-Qarnayn’s policy or define حُسْنًا as عفو.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-42:48\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The Messenger is told he is not a keeper over those who turn away, while Dhu al-Qarnayn receives an explicit choice about how to treat the people he encounters. This may distinguish proclamation from delegated earthly rule.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"42:48\",\"ar\":\"فَإِنْ أَعْرَضُوا۟ فَمَآ أَرْسَلْنَٰكَ عَلَيْهِمْ حَفِيظًا ۖ إِنْ عَلَيْكَ إِلَّا ٱلْبَلَٰغُ\"}],\"missing\":\"The role contrast is visible in the wording, but no supplied passage explicitly compares Dhu al-Qarnayn’s commission with the Messenger’s limits. A passage defining the scope of Dhu al-Qarnayn’s authority could sharpen it.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-43:38\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The focus names the sun’s western setting point, and its later expedition reaches the rising place; 43:38 uses the dual eastern points to express extreme distance. These may belong to a Quranic image of far horizons around Dhu al-Qarnayn’s itinerary.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:90\",\"ar\":\"مَطْلِعَ ٱلشَّمْسِ\"},{\"ref\":\"43:38\",\"ar\":\"بُعْدَ ٱلْمَشْرِقَيْنِ\"}],\"missing\":\"The verses do not explicitly link the two eastern points to Dhu al-Qarnayn or explain whether the dual title refers to horizons. Lexical evidence about ٱلْمَشْرِقَيْنِ and the title could establish or rule out that connection.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-44:22\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-44:28\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-45:31\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-46:15\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-46:23\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-48:16\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Both passages present alternatives with consequences, but 48:16 concerns a military call, contrasting obedience and turning away; the focus gives Dhu al-Qarnayn a choice about treatment of a people.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"48:16\",\"ar\":\"تُقَٰتِلُونَهُمْ أَوْ يُسْلِمُونَ ۖ فَإِن تُطِيعُوا۟ يُؤْتِكُمُ ٱللَّهُ أَجْرًا حَسَنًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s paired إِمَّا branches invite comparison with another Quranic either-or framing involving a people.\",\"limits\":\"The options in 48:16 describe a military outcome, not a ruler’s discretionary policy toward a community.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"X1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s إِمَّا branches are resolved by the next two ayat’s أَمَّا branches: punishment applies to the one who does wrong, while the one who believes and acts well receives the best reward and an easy course. The choice is conditional on conduct, not an undifferentiated choice between severity and leniency.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"أَمَّا مَن ظَلَمَ فَسَوْفَ نُعَذِّبُهُۥ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:88\",\"ar\":\"وَأَمَّا مَنْ ءَامَنَ وَعَمِلَ صَٰلِحًۭا فَلَهُۥ جَزَآءً ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ\"}],\"activation\":\"The near-verbatim repetition of the two-part frame at the beginning of 18:87–88 answers the alternatives spoken in 18:86.\",\"limits\":\"The answer clarifies the case distinction but does not specify every practical form of punishment or goodness.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X2\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The sun’s movement links two parts of the surah: its rising and setting around the sleepers’ cave are called signs of God, and Dhu al-Qarnayn’s journey is narrated through setting and rising endpoints. His sunset scene belongs to a surah-wide horizon motif.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:17\",\"ar\":\"وَتَرَى ٱلشَّمْسَ إِذَا طَلَعَت تَّزَٰوَرُ عَن كَهْفِهِمْ ذَاتَ ٱلْيَمِينِ وَإِذَا غَرَبَت تَّقْرِضُهُمْ ذَاتَ ٱلشِّمَالِ وَهُمْ فِى فَجْوَةٍۢ مِّنْهُ ۚ ذَٰلِكَ مِنْ ءَايَٰتِ ٱللَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:90\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَطْلِعَ ٱلشَّمْسِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names the sun’s setting place, while 18:17 and 18:90 describe its setting and rising elsewhere in the surah.\",\"limits\":\"The motif does not establish that the sun literally enters a spring or that the passages make the same claim about its movement.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X3\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The repeated حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا threshold formula links Dhu al-Qarnayn’s successive journeys with Moses and his companion’s staged encounters. In both travel sequences an arrival opens a new encounter and action; the focus’s first endpoint turns from observing the sun to finding a people and receiving a decision.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:71\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا رَكِبَا فِى ٱلسَّفِينَةِ خَرَقَهَا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:74\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا لَقِيَا غُلَٰمًۭا فَقَتَلَهُۥ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:77\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَآ أَتَيَآ أَهْلَ قَرْيَةٍ ٱسْتَطْعَمَآ أَهْلَهَا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ وَجَدَهَا تَغْرُبُ فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both narrative journeys use repeated arrival thresholds before discoveries, actions, and speech.\",\"limits\":\"The shared narrative frame does not make the two travelers’ knowledge, authority, or tests equivalent.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
